  "verbalization": "Thalidomide's potential role in hematologic cancer may be mediated through its ability to induce demyelination, which is linked to Nelarabine, an antineoplastic agent that treats hematologic cancer. This pathway highlights a connection between Thalidomide and cancer treatment through a chain of biological effects, where Thalidomide initiates a cascade that ultimately targets malignant cells. Similarly, Thalidomide's involvement in causing arterial embolism, which is linked to Doxorubicin or Epirubicin, both of which are antineoplastic agents used in the treatment of hematologic cancer, further supports the notion that Thalidomide may contribute to cancer therapy through indirect mechanisms. The shared classification of Thalidomide and these agents as antineoplastic agents and organic compounds underscores a recurring theme in their biological roles, suggesting a broader mechanism of action that involves the modulation of cellular processes critical to cancer development.",
